  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "## 12.4.0\n\n### Features\n- `Markdown.Extensions` takes Markdig extensions without subclassing the control: they are added to the parse pipeline after the supported ones, and changing the collection parses the text again. `ConfigurePipeline` still runs last.\n- `AppBuilder.ConfigureMarkdown(options => ...)` adds Markdig extensions to every `Markdown` control in the app through the new `MarkdownOptions.Extensions`. The actions run during app setup, before any control exists, in the order they were registered and all on the same options, so packages that each add an extension combine. A control adds them after the supported extensions and before its own `Extensions`. `MarkdownSerializer` does not read them.\n- `MarkdownSerializer` gains a constructor that takes Markdig extensions, `new MarkdownSerializer(options, codeHighlighter, extensions)`, and an `Extensions` property listing them. They affect reading only.\n- `<a id=\"name\"></a>` and `<a name=\"name\"></a>` in text name their paragraph as a bookmark (`Block.Bookmarks`), so `[x](#name)` navigates to it; the tag stays raw and writes back unchanged. A bookmark from another format (a Word bookmark) is written as `<a id=\"name\"></a>` at the start of its paragraph or heading.\n- Links reach headings by their GitHub slug: `[Setup](#getting-started)` scrolls to `## Getting Started`, and repeated headings are told apart as `#intro`, `#intro-1`, `#intro-2`. The slug is computed from the heading's current text when the link is followed, so it follows every edit. The control's `RequestNavigate` handler navigates to a bookmark, a heading or a note by label and marks the request handled; a fragment that names none of them still bubbles to the application.",
